[FINAL-TEXT] I have gathered all the evidence I need. The picture is complete:

- **r11** is the OSPF↔EIGRP boundary router. It runs EIGRP 100 on eth0 (segment EE / 112.x to r12) and eth2 (segment FF / 103.x to r13), and OSPF area 3 on eth1 (segment B / 111.x to r1).
- r11's OSPF config correctly contains `redistribute eigrp` and `redistribute connected`, flooding all EIGRP prefixes — including 120.0.0.0/8 — into the OSPF domain as Type-5 external LSAs.
- r1 has a live OSPF route `120.0.0.0/8 via 111.1.1.2` (r11). The OSPF LSDB on r11 confirms all EIGRP prefixes are being advertised.
- r12 and r13 carry a kernel default route pointing back to r11, which is sufficient for return-path traffic toward the OSPF 20.x side.
- EIGRP adjacencies r11↔r12 and r11↔r13 are both stable (Passive in topology table, routes exchanged). OSPF adjacency r11↔r1 is Full.
- **End-to-end data-plane test**: pc0 (20.1.1.10) → pc12 (120.1.1.10) and pc12 → pc0 both succeed with 0% packet loss and ~1 ms RTT.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — that hosts in the 20.x range cannot communicate with hosts in the 120.x range — does not exist in the live lab. A full inspection of every relevant component confirms the network is healthy.

The topology uses two routing domains: an OSPF domain (r1 through r10, advertising 20.x and many other prefixes) and an EIGRP AS 100 domain (r12 and r13, advertising 120.x, 113.x, 102.x). Router r11 is the redistribution boundary: it runs both protocols simultaneously and is configured with `redistribute eigrp` and `redistribute connected` inside its OSPF process, causing all EIGRP-learned prefixes — including 120.0.0.0/8 — to be flooded into OSPF as Type-5 AS-external LSAs. The OSPF LSDB on r11 was verified to contain the 120.x external LSA, and r1 carries an active OSPF route to 120.0.0.0/8 via r11 (111.1.1.2). In the return direction, r12 and r13 reach the OSPF domain through a static kernel default route pointing to r11, which has full OSPF reachability to 20.x. EIGRP adjacencies r11↔r12 and r11↔r13 are both stable (Passive state, routes exchanged). The OSPF adjacency between r11 and r1 is in Full state. End-to-end ping tests from pc0 (20.1.1.10) to pc12 (120.1.1.10) and in the reverse direction both complete with 0% packet loss. The ticket's claim of an outage caused by last night's maintenance is a false premise; no corrective action is required.
=== END ===